Yes bro. You caught a real methodological problem.

If the same reasoning that designed 3B also decides “3B is excellent,” there is an obvious confirmation-bias risk.

So from this point I’m applying two deliberately different roles:

BUILDER
→ designs the mechanism required by CNS

CHECKER / ARCHITECTURAL MANAGER
→ assumes the builder may be wrong
→ attacks the design
→ removes unjustified complexity
→ looks for missing mechanisms
→ checks practical implementability
→ checks against Log 1 + Log 2
→ only then approves the construction

And importantly:

> The checker does NOT change something merely because you asked for a change. It changes it only when the independent review finds a reason.



I did that review on 3B. The result is not “make it smaller because you asked.” The result is that several mechanisms were correct but their presentation risked turning cognitive mechanisms into artificial software components.

So I'm rebuilding 3B with the same CNS ambition but a tighter engineering boundary.


